WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:18.480 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it is May 7th and I've already played 25  new games this year  and aside from 2 or 3 of them they've  all been fantastic the last few months  one doubtedly go down as one of the  strongest openings to a year in the  mediums history but when I look back on  it and not just surprised by the general  quality of recent titles I'm also struck  by the sheer variety in one game I  fought my way through mythical Japanese  beasts in the next I hunted massive  robotic dinosaurs and yet another I  descended to earth with a squad of  androids clearing the way for the humans  we left back on the moon but just the  other day I started out last - it tells  a gory morbid supernatural tale about  fanatical cults in the American  Southwest it's tense and it's foreboding  at certain points it's downright  terrifying all in all it's a fairly  miserable experience yet I can't wait to  finish it this is always fascinating to  me why do I play games and I'm not  enjoying why do I spend time with games  that don't thrill me why do I pour hours  into entertainment that isn't fun in the  traditional sense I think we all do this  sometimes and I think recent games such  as outlast - a night in the woods  everything and what remains of Edith  Finch are important reminders for me and  for everyone that video games can be  much more than just fun  [Music]  so let's get something out of the way  right up front it's pretty tough to  define fun especially when it comes to  video games because we all play games  for different reasons maybe it's for a  sense of accomplishment  maybe it's an escape from our daily  routines or maybe it's for a sense of  community as we catch up with friends  and bond over our shared hobby but on a  basic fundamental level there's a  psychological concept that I want to use  it's called a flow State have you ever  played a game for what felt like 10  minutes but was really two hours have  you ever gone into autopilot when  fighting a really difficult boss you  were probably in a flow State it's that  fine line between excitement boredom and  anticipation and when you think back on  some of your favorite games chances are  they were good at placing you in this  state whether it's through fluid  mechanics well-placed gameplay or a good  balance between challenge and reward  developers often strive to encourage  this flow state if they do that they can  then build upon it with more complex  rules systems or storylines certain  games come to mind when talking about  this more traditional kind of fun Super  Mario 64 Super Smash Bros and the vast  majority of first-person shooters but  his games have evolved over the years  and creators have gotten better at  making them our definition of fun has  wavered and widened and this is a great  thing now I play games that are fun but  also games that can pull me out of my  comfort zone I play games that keep me  open-minded games that provides  something I can't find anywhere else  maybe this is why I love good horror  games with all their atmosphere and  psychological trauma they envelop me  more than any other genre they also  provide more agency than horror movies  which separate me from the events  unfolding in Halloween Michael Myers is  chasing other people in outlast -  deranged villagers are chasing me  think back on movies you've seen  throughout your life haven't you ever  finished watching a film and said that  was great but I felt awful the whole  time I felt this way about the revenant  the shining and Mulholland Drive they  led me through uncomfortable situations  they sometimes made me feel ill but  that's exactly what the filmmakers  wanted because there's value in that I  think games in their own way can  communicate stories and emotions we  might not otherwise feel we can  experience a situation in the shoes of  someone else someone broke in what they  wrecked the place through my typewriter  out the window we might not reach that  flow state that I mentioned that more  comforting sense of fun but that doesn't  make these games any less rewarding look  no further than that dragon cancer this  is a tragic powerful sometimes beautiful  story about parents doing their best to  raise a son with terminal cancer using  the interactivity of the medium that  dragon cancer shows young Joel Greene's  perilous journey through illness  recovery and his eventual death some  have said this game would function  better as a movie but that dragon  cancers creator Ryan Greene recognized  the value of agency inherent to video  games he could create a world for people  to not just see but also inhabit no  interactive spaces don't always have to  compel players to go on to the next  action or activity they they can help us  create a space where the player can  stand still and once the player is in  control of the pacing of the narrative  they can linger and then as the designer  we can acknowledge what they care about  and reward their presence  other games deliver negative emotions  without such a linear story take  something like XCOM to in its own way  this game makes use of much more than  traditional fun through turn-based  combat deep strategy and extreme  difficulty XCOM to conveys the pain of  failure as our favorite soldier dies  because of a simple mistake but it also  encourages us to roll with the punches  to make the best of failure and move  forward regardless then you have papers  please this game places you in the role  of immigrations officer in almost every  step of the way  confronts you with difficult moral  decisions you examine immigration papers  deciding who to let through who to  reject and in the case of falsified  information who to detain it gets even  more difficult when immigrants clearly  don't have the right papers yet they  have awful stories to tell about their  former homes  it would be easy to let them through out  of compassion but your salary is  contingent upon your performance and you  have your own family at home to feed as  well and this conversation would not be  complete without the puzzle game the  witness it is in no way fun  yes it's rewarding to the tenth degree  when you've spent hours trying to solve  a single puzzle only to discover the  solution right under your nose but I  would never call the witness fun and I  will never play it again yet it's one of  my favorite games in recent memory and  that's because the witness more than any  other game I can think of trust that  players are intelligent and it  communicates its ideas eloquently  helping you accrue knowledge as you  investigate this mysterious island and  it is knowledge itself that becomes your  key for getting around now don't get me  wrong I don't want every game to  communicate negative emotions or the  utility of failure or the importance of  learning one of my favorite games ever  timesplitters 2 is the epitome of fun  it's a shooter with goofy humor and  ham-fisted dialogue it uses time-travel  to justify having characters and weapons  from the Wild West the far future Soviet  Russia and 1930s Chicago there are evil  snowmen and undead priests I love it  with all of my heart  [Music]  yeah  but there are more intelligent people  making games by the day and many more  intelligent people playing them and  there will always be room for the next  zelda persona or Ghost Recon but that  doesn't mean we should discount games  such as a night in the woods everything  or what remains of Edith Finch simply  because they don't deliver fun in the  traditional sense personally I want the  medium to keep questioning and hopefully  defying what it means to be a video game  I want games to morph and twist and get  weirder and convey new ideas that fly in  the face of tradition I want developers  to be bolder with each new game they  create I want players to play these  games and to acknowledge that the medium  can be about much more than just fun  [Music]  hey everyone thanks so much for watching  episode 6 of reboot if you've been  watching this far then you know next  week Jake and I will be answering  questions about episode 6 and taking  suggestions for future episodes so in  the meantime your YouTube comments and  tweet at us Mike McCarty and Jake Decker  and maybe will feature your on show I'll  see you next week  you

